(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-01"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Compound angle formulae give the sine, cosine or tangent of the sum or the difference of two angles in terms of the sines, cosines and tangents of the angles themselves. They are proved first for the cosine of a difference, and all the others follow by changing the signs or by using the complementary angle. They are the base of all the later identities: double angles, factorisation and the angles of a triangle.","f":[{"x":"cos(A - B) = cos A*cos B + sin A*sin B","n":"Cosine of a difference. For a sum: cos(A + B) = cos A*cos B - sin A*sin B.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.1, p.36"},{"x":"sin(A - B) = sin A*cos B - cos A*sin B","n":"Sine of a difference. For a sum: sin(A + B) = sin A*cos B + cos A*sin B.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.1, p.36"},{"x":"tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 - tan A*tan B)","n":"Tangent of a sum. For a difference: tan(A - B) = (tan A - tan B)/(1 + tan A*tan B).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.1, p.36"}],"w":["Cosine changes the sign between the terms: cos(A + B) has a minus, cos(A − B) has a plus. Sine keeps the sign.","In the tangent formulas the sign in the denominator is opposite to the sign in the numerator."]},"3.2":{"s":"Allied angles are angles that differ from a multiple of 90° by θ, such as 90° − θ, 180° + θ or 360° − θ. Their trigonometric functions can be written in terms of θ. Use two rules: decide whether the function changes to its co-function, which happens when the angle is an odd multiple of 90°, and decide the sign from the quadrant in which the angle falls.","f":[{"x":"sin(pi - theta) = sin(theta), cos(pi - theta) = -cos(theta)","n":"Second quadrant. Also tan(pi - theta) = -tan(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.2, p.40"},{"x":"sin(pi/2 - theta) = cos(theta), cos(pi/2 - theta) = sin(theta)","n":"Complementary angles. Also tan(pi/2 - theta) = cot(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.2, p.40"}],"w":["Sign comes from the quadrant of the original angle, as if θ were a small positive angle.","With 90° or 270° the function changes (sin to cos); with 180° or 360° it stays the same."]},"3.3":{"s":"The double angle formulae write the functions of 2θ in terms of θ. The sine is twice the product of sin θ and cos θ. The cosine has three equal forms, using cos² θ − sin² θ, 2 cos² θ − 1 or 1 − 2 sin² θ. The tangent is given by 2 tan θ over 1 − tan² θ. The cosine forms can be rearranged to get the half angle results.","f":[{"x":"sin(2*theta) = 2*sin(theta)*cos(theta) = 2*tan(theta)/(1 + tan(theta)^2)","n":"Sine of the double angle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.3, p.43"},{"x":"cos(2*theta) = cos(theta)^2 - sin(theta)^2 = 2*cos(theta)^2 - 1 = 1 - 2*sin(theta)^2","n":"Cosine of the double angle; also (1 - tan(theta)^2)/(1 + tan(theta)^2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.3, p.43"},{"x":"tan(2*theta) = 2*tan(theta)/(1 - tan(theta)^2)","n":"Tangent of the double angle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.3, p.43"}],"w":["cos 2θ has three forms: pick the one that suits the problem (only sin, only cos, or only tan).","sin 2θ is not 2 sin θ: the factor cos θ must be there."]},"3.4":{"s":"Factorisation formulae change a sum or a difference of two sines or cosines into a product, which often makes an expression easier to simplify. They come from the compound angle formulae by putting A = (C + D)/2 and B = (C − D)/2. The reverse formulae, which change a product into a sum or a difference, are called defactorisation formulae.","f":[{"x":"sin C + sin D = 2*sin((C + D)/2)*cos((C - D)/2)","n":"Sum of sines. Difference: sin C - sin D = 2*cos((C + D)/2)*sin((C - D)/2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.4, p.48"},{"x":"cos C + cos D = 2*cos((C + D)/2)*cos((C - D)/2)","n":"Sum of cosines. Difference: cos C - cos D = -2*sin((C + D)/2)*sin((C - D)/2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.4, p.48"}],"w":["cos C − cos D has a minus sign in front: −2 sin((C + D)/2) sin((C − D)/2).","Only a sum or difference of two sines or two cosines at a time can be factorised in this way."]},"3.5":{"s":"When A, B and C are the angles of a triangle, they add up to 180°, that is π radians. So A + B = π − C, and the functions of A + B turn into functions of C through the allied angle rules: sin(A + B) = sin C and cos(A + B) = −cos C. With the compound angle, double angle and factorisation formulae this gives many identities for the angles of a triangle.","w":["Use A + B + C = π first to turn a sum of two angles into the third angle.","cos(A + B) = −cos C: the sign changes for the cosine and the tangent, not for the sine."]}};
